# (topic, stem, correct, [wrong x3], rationale)
Q = [
# ---- GAS / complications (8)
("Strep pharyngitis","A 7-year-old has sudden sore throat, fever of 39.1°C, tender anterior cervical nodes, and palatal petechiae. No cough or runny nose. The rapid strep test is negative. What should the nurse anticipate next?",
 "Send a backup throat culture",["Reassure the family that the illness is viral","Start amoxicillin without further testing","Draw an ASO titer to confirm strep"],
 "A negative rapid antigen test in a symptomatic child aged 3 or older needs a backup throat culture. ASO shows past strep, not acute infection."),
("Strep pharyngitis","Which group of findings most strongly suggests a VIRAL sore throat rather than group A strep?",
 "Cough, rhinorrhea, hoarseness, and oral ulcers",["Tonsillar exudate and fever","Palatal petechiae and headache","Tender anterior cervical lymph nodes"],
 "\"Looks like a COLD → not strep.\" Exudate alone does not prove GAS because EBV and other viruses can cause it."),
("Strep pharyngitis","A 30-kg child with confirmed GAS cannot reliably take oral medication. What is the correct single IM dose of benzathine penicillin G?",
 "1.2 million units",["300,000 units","600,000 units","2.4 million units"],
 "27 kg is the dividing line: under 27 kg = 600,000 units; 27 kg or more = 1.2 million units."),
("Strep pharyngitis","A parent asks why their child must finish all 10 days of amoxicillin for strep throat when they already feel better. The best response is that completing the course:",
 "Reduces the risk of acute rheumatic fever",["Prevents poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","Prevents infectious mononucleosis","Is needed only if the child has scarlet fever"],
 "\"Penicillin protects the Heart, not the Kidney.\" Treatment prevents ARF; antibiotics do not reliably prevent PSGN."),
("Strep complications","Two weeks after a strep infection, a child has tea-colored urine, puffy eyes, and an elevated blood pressure. The nurse suspects:",
 "Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis",["Acute rheumatic fever","Peritonsillar abscess","Kawasaki disease"],
 "PSGN = \"Cola, Puffy, Pressure\": dark urine, edema, and high BP after strep."),
("Strep complications","A 15-year-old has severe throat pain worse on the right, a muffled \"hot potato\" voice, trismus, and a uvula deviated to the LEFT. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
 "Right peritonsillar abscess",["Left peritonsillar abscess","Epiglottitis","Viral pharyngitis"],
 "The uvula runs AWAY from the abscess. Deviation to the left means the abscess is on the right."),
("Strep complications","A 16-year-old has fever, exudative pharyngitis, marked fatigue, posterior cervical nodes, and splenomegaly. What is the priority teaching?",
 "Avoid contact sports until the spleen is evaluated",["Take amoxicillin for 10 days","Stay home from school for 21 days","Expect a tonsillectomy"],
 "\"Front nodes = Strep, Back nodes = Mono.\" An enlarged spleen can rupture, so avoid contact sports."),
("Strep complications","An elevated ASO titer in a child is best interpreted as evidence of:",
 "A preceding streptococcal infection",["An acute strep throat that needs treatment today","A confirmed diagnosis of rheumatic fever","Epstein-Barr virus infection"],
 "ASO = \"Already Strep Occurred.\" It supports prior exposure but does not diagnose ARF by itself."),
# ---- Pneumonia (6)
("Pneumonia","A 6-week-old is afebrile with a staccato cough and tachypnea. The infant had conjunctivitis at 2 weeks of age. The most likely organism is:",
 "Chlamydia trachomatis",["Group B Streptococcus","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus"],
 "Chlamydia's 3 C's: Choppy (staccato) cough, Cool (afebrile), Conjunctivitis first. Classic at 1–3 months."),
("Pneumonia","An 11-year-old has 10 days of gradually worsening headache, malaise, and dry cough but looks well. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?",
 "Azithromycin",["Amoxicillin","Cephalexin","Oseltamivir"],
 "\"My School = Mycoplasma.\" A gradual, atypical pattern in a school-age child is treated with a macrolide."),
("Pneumonia","A fully immunized 4-year-old has abrupt high fever, tachypnea, and focal crackles in the right lower lobe. The child is stable for outpatient care. The expected treatment is:",
 "High-dose amoxicillin, about 90 mg/kg/day divided BID",["Azithromycin alone","No antibiotics, supportive care only","Daily IM ceftriaxone for 10 days"],
 "Typical bacterial pneumonia (S. pneumoniae) → amoxicillin. A macrolide is not an automatic substitute."),
("Pneumonia","A 6-year-old has fever and abdominal pain with a benign abdominal exam. What should the nurse do next?",
 "Assess respiratory rate and auscultate the lungs",["Arrange an abdominal ultrasound","Reassure the family that it is a stomach virus","Give ibuprofen and discharge"],
 "Lower-lobe pneumonia can present as belly pain. Always examine the lungs in a febrile child with abdominal complaints."),
("Pneumonia","A child who developed severe pneumonia shortly after influenza is most likely infected with which organism, in addition to pneumococcus?",
 "Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA",["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Chlamydia trachomatis","Group B Streptococcus"],
 "After the flu → think Staph (including MRSA) and pneumococcus."),
("Pneumonia","After 72 hours of amoxicillin for pneumonia, a child is still febrile and slightly worse. The best nursing action is to:",
 "Report for reassessment of complications, adherence, dose, or another diagnosis",["Continue the same plan for 7 more days","Add an over-the-counter cough suppressant","Switch to a nebulized bronchodilator"],
 "No improvement in 48–72 hours → reassess for empyema/effusion, the wrong organism, adherence, or a foreign body."),
# ---- Bronchitis/other resp (5)
("Other respiratory","A 9-year-old has a cough after a cold, scattered rhonchi, no focal findings, and yellow sputum. The appropriate management is:",
 "Supportive care; antibiotics are not routinely indicated",["Amoxicillin for 10 days","Azithromycin for 5 days","Chest x-ray and CBC before deciding"],
 "Acute bronchitis is usually viral. Purulent sputum alone does not justify antibiotics."),
("Other respiratory","A 4-month-old has bronchiolitis with wheeze, mild retractions, an SpO2 of 94%, and is feeding adequately. Which intervention is appropriate?",
 "Gentle nasal suction, hydration support, and monitoring",["Scheduled albuterol nebulizers","Oral prednisolone","Amoxicillin"],
 "Bronchiolitis = 3 S's: Suction, Sips, Saturation. No routine albuterol, steroids, or antibiotics."),
("Other respiratory","A child has abrupt fever, chills, headache, and severe muscle aches. These findings most suggest:",
 "Influenza",["Common cold","Allergic rhinitis","Acute bacterial sinusitis"],
 "\"The flu hits like a truck.\" A cold is gradual, with a runny nose and mild symptoms."),
("Other respiratory","Which presentation meets the criteria for acute bacterial sinusitis?",
 "Daytime cough and nasal drainage for more than 10 days without improvement",["Green nasal mucus for 3 days","Sneezing, itchy eyes, and clear drainage","Clear rhinorrhea for 5 days with low-grade fever"],
 "\"10 days, Double-dip, 39 for 3.\" Colored mucus alone is not enough."),
("Other respiratory","The parent of an 8-month-old with a cold asks about giving honey for the cough. The nurse should respond:",
 "Honey should not be given until after age 1",["A teaspoon of honey at bedtime is safe","Honey works best mixed with an OTC cough syrup","Honey is safe if warmed first"],
 "Honey is only for children over 1 year (botulism risk). Avoid OTC cough/cold combinations in young children."),
# ---- Asthma (8)
("Asthma","An 8-year-old has symptoms 3 days/week, wakes at night 3 times/month, uses albuterol 3 days/week, has minor activity limitation, and an FEV1 of 85%. Classify the severity:",
 "Mild persistent",["Intermittent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],
 "More than 2 days/week but not daily, and 3–4 nights/month = mild persistent."),
("Asthma","A 9-year-old has daytime symptoms 2 days/week, but wakes at night twice a week. The FEV1 is 82%. Classify the severity:",
 "Moderate persistent",["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Severe persistent"],
 "\"Worst feature wins.\" Night waking more than once a week (but not nightly) = moderate persistent."),
("Asthma","A child is newly diagnosed with mild persistent asthma. Which daily controller medication does the nurse expect?",
 "Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid",["Albuterol as needed only","Salmeterol alone","Daily oral prednisone"],
 "Mild persistent = Step 2: daily low-dose ICS. \"ICS = Insurance.\""),
("Asthma","Which instruction is correct for a child using an inhaled corticosteroid?",
 "Rinse the mouth and spit after each use",["Use it only during asthma attacks","Stop it once symptoms improve","Use a spacer with the dry-powder inhaler"],
 "Rinse and spit reduces thrush. ICS is used daily. Dry-powder inhalers do NOT use spacers."),
("Asthma","During an asthma exacerbation, a child's wheezing becomes quieter, but the child is more drowsy with poor air movement. The nurse recognizes this as:",
 "Impending respiratory failure",["Improvement from the bronchodilator","A sign the attack is ending","Normal fatigue after treatment"],
 "\"A silent chest is a scream.\" Less wheeze with worse air entry and fatigue means respiratory failure."),
("Asthma","A child's personal best peak flow is 300 L/min. Today's reading is 180 L/min. Which zone is this?",
 "Yellow zone",["Green zone","Red zone","Cannot be determined"],
 "180/300 = 60%. Green is 80% or more, yellow 50–79%, red under 50%."),
("Asthma","Which teaching about montelukast is most important?",
 "Report mood or behavior changes, including suicidal thoughts",["Use it for acute attacks","It replaces the albuterol inhaler","Rinse the mouth after each dose"],
 "\"Montelukast = Mood warning\" (boxed warning). It is not a rescue medication."),
("Asthma","Which statement about salmeterol (a LABA) for asthma is correct?",
 "It must be combined with an inhaled corticosteroid, never used alone",["It is the preferred rescue inhaler","It is first-line for intermittent asthma","It can replace the inhaled corticosteroid"],
 "\"LABA never Alone.\" Salmeterol is not a rescue drug."),
# ---- Croup / epiglottitis (5)
("Croup","A 2-year-old has a barking cough, hoarse voice, and inspiratory stridor only when crying. Symptoms are worse at night. What does the nurse anticipate?",
 "A single dose of oral dexamethasone",["Albuterol nebulizer","Amoxicillin","A routine neck x-ray"],
 "\"Dex for all.\" Mild croup = dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg once. Croup is a clinical diagnosis."),
("Croup","A child with croup and stridor at rest receives nebulized epinephrine. What should the nurse plan next?",
 "Observe for 2–4 hours for return of symptoms",["Discharge home once stridor resolves","Give antibiotics","Start scheduled albuterol"],
 "Epinephrine works fast but wears off. \"Watch 2–4 hours before the door.\""),
("Croup","A 4-year-old has abrupt high fever, drooling, a muffled voice, and is sitting in a tripod position without a barking cough. The nurse's priority is to:",
 "Keep the child calm with the parent and prepare for emergency airway care",["Inspect the throat with a tongue blade","Obtain a throat culture","Send the child to radiology alone for a neck x-ray"],
 "Epiglottitis = 4 D's (Drooling, Dysphagia, Dysphonia, Distress). Do not inspect the throat. Airway comes first."),
("Croup","Croup is most commonly caused by which organism, and in which age group?",
 "Parainfluenza virus; about 6 months to 3 years",["RSV; under 2 months","Group A strep; ages 5–15","Bordetella pertussis; adolescents"],
 "\"Para-croup.\" The small airway in young children narrows easily."),
("Croup","A toddler with presumed croup has high fever, looks toxic, and does not improve with dexamethasone and epinephrine. The nurse suspects:",
 "Bacterial tracheitis",["Mild viral croup","Allergic rhinitis","Bronchiolitis"],
 "High fever, toxicity, and poor response to standard croup treatment suggest bacterial tracheitis."),
# ---- Pertussis / foreign body (5)
("Pertussis","A 6-week-old has episodes of apnea and turning blue after coughing, but no whoop. An older sibling has had a cough for 3 weeks. The most likely diagnosis is:",
 "Pertussis",["Croup","Allergic rhinitis","Epiglottitis"],
 "Infants with pertussis often have apnea and cyanosis WITHOUT a whoop. Look for a coughing contact."),
("Pertussis","Coughing fits, an inspiratory whoop, and vomiting after coughing describe which stage of pertussis?",
 "Paroxysmal",["Catarrhal","Convalescent","Incubation"],
 "\"Cold → Cough → Calm\" = Catarrhal → Paroxysmal → Convalescent."),
("Pertussis","A child with pertussis started azithromycin. When may the child return to school?",
 "After 5 full days of effective antibiotics",["After 24 hours of antibiotics","When the cough completely stops","After 10 days of antibiotics"],
 "\"5 days on drugs, 21 days without\" (untreated: 21 days after cough onset)."),
("Foreign body","A 2-year-old started coughing suddenly while eating peanuts. Now there is wheezing and decreased breath sounds on the right only. The chest x-ray is normal. The next step is:",
 "Prompt specialist referral for bronchoscopy",["Reassurance because the x-ray is normal","An albuterol trial for new asthma","Amoxicillin for pneumonia"],
 "\"Sudden + one Side\" = foreign body. Food is radiolucent, so a normal x-ray does not rule it out."),
("Foreign body","Which statement about foreign body aspiration is correct?",
 "Blind finger sweeps should be avoided",["A normal chest x-ray rules it out","It always follows a witnessed choking episode","Bilateral wheeze is the classic finding"],
 "Avoid blind finger sweeps. The choking history may be absent, and findings are usually unilateral."),
# ---- AOM (7)
("Otitis media","Which otoscopic finding best supports a diagnosis of acute otitis media?",
 "Moderate to severe bulging of the tympanic membrane",["A red TM in a crying child","Ear tugging","Clear fluid behind the TM without symptoms"],
 "\"Bulge = AOM. Red alone = Rubbish.\" Crying reddens the TM, and tugging is nonspecific."),
("Otitis media","A 15-month-old has nonsevere AOM in BOTH ears with a temperature of 38.3°C. The recommended plan is:",
 "Antibiotics",["Observation for 48–72 hours","Ear drops only","Referral for ear tubes"],
 "Under 2 years with both ears affected → antibiotics, even when nonsevere."),
("Otitis media","A 3-year-old has nonsevere AOM in one ear, mild pain for less than 48 hours, and a temperature of 38.2°C. Which option is appropriate?",
 "Pain control with observation and follow-up in 48–72 hours, or antibiotics, by shared decision",["Antibiotics are mandatory","Immediate ENT referral","Withhold pain medication to monitor symptoms"],
 "Age 2+ and nonsevere → observation or antibiotics. ALWAYS treat the pain."),
("Otitis media","A 2-year-old has AOM along with purulent conjunctivitis. Which antibiotic is preferred?",
 "Amoxicillin-clavulanate",["Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Topical eye drops only"],
 "Augmentin 3 C's: amox in the last 30 days, Conjunctivitis plus otitis (nontypeable H. flu), Can't clear."),
("Otitis media","What is the first-line amoxicillin dose for AOM?",
 "80–90 mg/kg/day divided BID",["50 mg/kg once daily","20 mg/kg/dose BID","25 mg/kg/day divided TID"],
 "High-dose amoxicillin for AOM differs from the GAS regimen (50 mg/kg once daily)."),
("Otitis media","A 4-year-old with nonsevere AOM is prescribed amoxicillin. What is the typical duration?",
 "7 days",["5 days","10 days","14 days"],
 "\"10–7–5\": under 2 or severe = 10 days; age 2–5 = 7 days; age 6 and older = 5–7 days."),
("Otitis media","A child being treated for AOM develops fever, redness and swelling behind the ear, and an ear that sticks out. The nurse suspects:",
 "Mastoiditis requiring urgent evaluation",["Otitis externa","Otitis media with effusion","Normal healing"],
 "Postauricular swelling and a protruding pinna = mastoiditis → urgent hospital/ENT care."),
# ---- Other ear / hearing (5)
("Ear and hearing","A child has amber fluid with an air-fluid level behind the TM, no pain, and no fever. The best plan is:",
 "Watchful waiting for about 3 months, with a hearing check if it persists",["Amoxicillin for 10 days","Decongestants and antihistamines","Immediate ear tube placement"],
 "OME = \"Fluid without Fire.\" No antibiotics, antihistamines, decongestants, or steroids."),
("Ear and hearing","A child who swims daily has ear pain that worsens when the tragus is pressed. Expected treatment is:",
 "Topical otic drops and keeping the ear dry",["Oral amoxicillin","Ear irrigation","Observation only"],
 "Otitis externa: \"Pull the ear, it hurts.\" Treat with topical therapy and keep the ear dry."),
("Ear and hearing","Which is an example of CONDUCTIVE hearing loss?",
 "Hearing loss from middle-ear fluid (OME)",["Damage to the cochlea","Auditory nerve damage","Congenital CMV damage to hair cells"],
 "Conductive = Cerumen and fluid (outer/middle ear). Sensorineural = Snail (cochlea) and nerve."),
("Ear and hearing","The 1–3–6 hearing guideline means:",
 "Screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3 months, intervene by 6 months",["Screen at 1 year, 3 years, and 6 years","1 test, 3 retests, 6-month follow-up","Screen at 1, 3, and 6 months"],
 "1–3–6 = screen, diagnose, early intervention."),
("Ear and hearing","Ear irrigation is CONTRAINDICATED for which foreign body?",
 "A button battery",["A small plastic bead with an intact TM","Hard cerumen with an intact TM","A small stone with an intact TM"],
 "Never irrigate the 3 B's: Battery, Beans (vegetable matter), Broken eardrum. A battery requires urgent removal."),
# ---- Eye (5)
("Eye","A child has intensely itchy eyes in both eyes, with watery, stringy discharge, during spring. What should the nurse teach?",
 "This is not contagious; use cool compresses and allergy eye drops",["Stay home until 24 hours of antibiotic drops","Start antibiotic ointment","Wear an eye patch"],
 "Allergic = Itchy. Not contagious. Reduce exposure; antihistamine/mast-cell stabilizer drops."),
("Eye","A toddler wakes with thick yellow discharge and eyelids glued shut. What else should the nurse assess?",
 "The ears, for concurrent otitis media",["Visual acuity with a Snellen chart","Lung sounds for wheezing","The skin for a sandpaper rash"],
 "Bacterial conjunctivitis + AOM (often nontypeable H. flu) → amoxicillin-clavulanate."),
("Eye","A child has had a firm, painless eyelid nodule for 3 weeks. The most likely diagnosis and initial care are:",
 "Chalazion; warm compresses and lid hygiene",["Hordeolum; oral antibiotics","Orbital cellulitis; IV antibiotics","Chalazion; squeeze to drain it"],
 "\"A Stye Stings, a Chalazion is Chill.\" A chalazion is sterile, so antibiotics are not routine. Never squeeze."),
("Eye","Which finding distinguishes orbital cellulitis from preseptal cellulitis?",
 "Pain with eye movement and proptosis",["Red, swollen eyelid","Fever","Recent sinus infection"],
 "Orbital = 4 P's: Pain with movement, Proptosis, Poor vision, Paralysis (restricted movement) → emergency IV antibiotics."),
("Eye","A teen who wears contact lenses has a painful red eye and light sensitivity. The nurse should advise:",
 "Remove the lenses and get urgent evaluation for keratitis",["Use steroid eye drops","Use over-the-counter redness-relief drops","Continue wearing lenses and use warm compresses"],
 "Contact lens + painful red eye = possible keratitis. Never use steroid drops on an undiagnosed red eye."),
# ---- Vision / neonatal / trauma (5)
("Vision","A child is nearsighted (myopic). Which statement is correct?",
 "Distant objects are blurry because light focuses in front of the retina",["Near objects are blurry because light focuses behind the retina","Both eyes have unequal refractive errors","The eyes are misaligned"],
 "\"MY-opia: I only see what's near MY face.\" Hyperopia = farsighted; anisometropia = unequal; strabismus = misaligned."),
("Vision","A 4-year-old reads the 20/50 line with each eye. What should the nurse do?",
 "Refer, because the passing threshold at age 4 is 20/40",["Pass the child, because 20/50 is normal at 4","Retest in 2 years","Refer only if the child has symptoms"],
 "AAPOS: age 3 = 20/50, age 4 = 20/40, age 5+ = 20/32. \"Child grows, number shrinks.\""),
("Vision","During a well-child exam, the nurse notes a white pupillary reflex in one eye. What is the priority?",
 "Prompt ophthalmology referral to evaluate for retinoblastoma",["Recheck at the next well visit","Reassure the family because the child has no pain","Start antibiotic eye drops"],
 "Leukocoria = retinoblastoma (or cataract) until proven otherwise. Absence of pain is not reassuring."),
("Vision","A 3-day-old has profuse purulent eye discharge with marked swelling (chemosis). The nurse suspects:",
 "Gonococcal conjunctivitis needing urgent systemic ceftriaxone",["Chlamydial conjunctivitis treated with topical ointment","Blocked tear duct","Allergic conjunctivitis"],
 "\"Gonorrhea Goes fast\" (days 2–5); it threatens the cornea. \"Chlamydia Comes later\" (days 5–12, oral treatment)."),
("Vision","A child gets a household cleaner splashed in the eye. What is the first action?",
 "Begin immediate, copious irrigation",["Take a full history first","Apply an eye patch","Check visual acuity before anything else"],
 "Chemical injury: irrigate FIRST. Do not delay irrigation for a history."),
# ---- Fetal circulation / CHD basics (5)
("Fetal circulation","In fetal circulation, the foramen ovale shunts blood from:",
 "Right atrium to left atrium",["Pulmonary artery to aorta","Umbilical vein past the liver","Left ventricle to right ventricle"],
 "Foramen Ovale = \"Open door between the atria\" (RA → LA). Ductus arteriosus = PA → aorta. Ductus venosus bypasses the liver."),
("Fetal circulation","What causes functional closure of the foramen ovale after birth?",
 "Increased left atrial pressure",["Increased pulmonary vascular resistance","Prostaglandin E1 release","Decreased systemic resistance"],
 "Lungs expand → PVR falls, cord clamping raises systemic resistance → left atrial pressure rises and pushes the flap closed."),
("CHD basics","Which congenital heart defect is CYANOTIC?",
 "Tetralogy of Fallot",["Atrial septal defect","Ventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus"],
 "Cyanotic = the 5 T's. The \"D's\" (ASD, VSD, PDA) are acyanotic left-to-right shunts."),
("CHD basics","Which finding is an early sign of heart failure in an infant?",
 "Sweating and tiring during feeds, with poor weight gain",["Marked ankle edema","Bradycardia","Weight gain from overeating"],
 "\"Feeding is the infant's treadmill.\" Peripheral edema is less prominent than in adults."),
("CHD basics","A large, uncorrected left-to-right shunt can eventually reverse to right-to-left flow with cyanosis. This is called:",
 "Eisenmenger physiology",["Tet spell","Coarctation","Physiologic splitting"],
 "Long-standing overcirculation → pulmonary hypertension → shunt reversal (Eisenmenger)."),
# ---- ASD/VSD (4)
("ASD/VSD","Which heart sound is the classic finding in an atrial septal defect?",
 "Wide, fixed split S2",["Continuous machinery murmur","Harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border","Diastolic murmur at the apex"],
 "ASD → \"Always Split, Doesn't change.\" Increased right-sided flow delays pulmonic valve closure."),
("ASD/VSD","A harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border is most consistent with:",
 "Ventricular septal defect",["Atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Still's murmur"],
 "VSD = \"Very loud.\" And a small VSD can be loud: \"Small hole, loud howl.\""),
("ASD/VSD","An infant with a large VSD had a normal newborn exam but develops tachypnea and poor feeding at 6 weeks. Why?",
 "Pulmonary vascular resistance fell, increasing left-to-right shunting",["The VSD grew larger","The ductus arteriosus reopened","The shunt reversed to right-to-left"],
 "As pulmonary resistance drops over the first weeks, more blood crosses to the lungs → heart failure."),
("ASD/VSD","Atrioventricular (AV) septal defect is most associated with:",
 "Down syndrome",["Marfan syndrome","Turner syndrome only","Kawasaki disease"],
 "AV canal defect ↔ Down syndrome; it can cause early heart failure."),
# ---- PDA / obstructive (4)
("PDA/obstructive","Which findings are classic for a significant patent ductus arteriosus?",
 "Continuous machinery murmur with bounding pulses",["Fixed split S2","Weak femoral pulses","Boot-shaped heart on x-ray"],
 "PDA = \"machinery.\" Wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses."),
("PDA/obstructive","A child has a higher blood pressure in the arms than in the legs, with weak, delayed femoral pulses. The nurse suspects:",
 "Coarctation of the aorta",["Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect","Pulmonary stenosis"],
 "Coarctation = \"Strong upstairs, weak downstairs.\""),
("PDA/obstructive","A teen faints while playing basketball. A systolic ejection murmur is heard. What is the most appropriate response?",
 "Hold sports clearance and arrange a cardiac evaluation",["Reassure, since syncope is common in teens","Clear for sports with more hydration","Recheck at next year's physical"],
 "\"Faints while running = Referral.\" Consider aortic stenosis or HCM."),
("PDA/obstructive","Which medication may be used to CLOSE a PDA in a selected premature infant?",
 "Indomethacin or ibuprofen",["Prostaglandin E1","Digoxin","Dexamethasone"],
 "\"Indomethacin Interrupts (closes); Prostaglandin keeps it Patent.\""),
# ---- Cyanotic (5)
("Cyanotic CHD","Which is NOT a component of Tetralogy of Fallot?",
 "Atrial septal defect",["Pulmonary stenosis","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Overriding aorta"],
 "TOF = PROV: Pulmonary stenosis, RVH, Overriding aorta, VSD (ventricular, not atrial)."),
("Cyanotic CHD","An infant with TOF suddenly becomes deeply cyanotic and breathes rapidly while crying. The nurse should first:",
 "Place the infant in a knee-chest position",["Lay the infant flat and supine","Start a feeding to calm the infant","Give albuterol"],
 "Tet spell → knee-chest (raises systemic resistance and pushes blood to the lungs), calm the infant, give O2, call for help."),
("Cyanotic CHD","A newborn with d-transposition of the great arteries is severely cyanotic. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?",
 "Prostaglandin E1 to keep the ductus open",["Indomethacin to close the ductus","Furosemide","Aspirin"],
 "TGA = \"Two separate circles\" that need mixing. Never close that PDA."),
("Cyanotic CHD","A nurse is caring for an infant on a prostaglandin E1 infusion. What is the priority to monitor for?",
 "Apnea",["Hypertension","Bradycardia only","Hyperglycemia"],
 "PGE1 requires monitored care because apnea can occur."),
("Cyanotic CHD","A 3-day-old with hypoplastic left heart syndrome becomes gray, poorly perfused, and in shock. Most likely cause:",
 "Closure of the ductus arteriosus",["Opening of the foramen ovale","Rising pulmonary resistance","Normal transition"],
 "HLHS is duct-dependent. Deterioration as the PDA closes is a key neonatal clue."),
# ---- Murmurs (4)
("Murmurs","A healthy 5-year-old has a soft, grade 2, musical systolic murmur that is louder lying down and softer standing. Growth is normal. This is most likely:",
 "Still's (innocent) murmur",["Ventricular septal defect","Aortic stenosis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],
 "Innocent = 7 S's. Still's is vibratory/musical and louder supine."),
("Murmurs","Which murmur characteristic is always considered pathologic?",
 "Diastolic timing",["Grade 2 intensity","Changes with position","Musical quality"],
 "\"Diastolic is always Dangerous.\" Exception to know: a venous hum is continuous and innocent."),
("Murmurs","A murmur is loud and accompanied by a palpable thrill. What is the Levine grade?",
 "Grade IV",["Grade II","Grade III","Grade VI"],
 "\"4 = you can FEEL it.\" Grade III is loud without a thrill; VI is heard with the stethoscope off the chest."),
("Murmurs","A teen's murmur gets LOUDER when standing and during Valsalva. This suggests:",
 "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy",["Still's murmur","Venous hum","Normal physiologic split"],
 "HCM gets louder with standing/Valsalva (less filling). Most innocent murmurs get softer."),
# ---- Diagnostics (3)
("Cardiac tests","Which test confirms the location and size of a suspected VSD?",
 "Echocardiogram with Doppler",["ECG","Chest x-ray","Pulse oximetry"],
 "\"Echo = Eyes (anatomy); ECG = Electricity.\" An ECG cannot show a hole."),
("Cardiac tests","A child has palpitations every day. Which test is most appropriate?",
 "Holter monitor",["Cardiac catheterization","Event monitor for several months","Echocardiogram only"],
 "\"Holter = Hold on for a day\" (frequent symptoms). \"Event = Every now and then\" (rare symptoms)."),
("Cardiac tests","After a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery, which assessment is the priority?",
 "Access-site bleeding and pulses distal to the site",["Bowel sounds","Pupil reactivity","Urine specific gravity"],
 "After a cath, monitor the site for bleeding and check distal pulses and perfusion."),
# ---- Kawasaki (4)
("Kawasaki","A 3-year-old has had fever for 6 days, both eyes red without discharge, a strawberry tongue with cracked lips, swollen red hands, and a rash. The nurse suspects:",
 "Kawasaki disease",["Scarlet fever","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Rheumatic fever"],
 "CRASH and BURN: fever 5+ days plus 4 of 5 (Conjunctivitis, Rash, Adenopathy, Strawberry tongue, Hands/feet)."),
("Kawasaki","The most serious complication of Kawasaki disease is:",
 "Coronary artery aneurysm",["Mitral valve damage","Kidney failure","Hearing loss"],
 "\"Kawasaki Crowns the Coronaries.\" (Rheumatic fever targets valves.)"),
("Kawasaki","Which treatment does the nurse expect for Kawasaki disease?",
 "IVIG 2 g/kg plus aspirin",["Amoxicillin plus ibuprofen","Oral prednisone only","Benzathine penicillin monthly"],
 "IVIG within the first 10 days reduces coronary complications. Kawasaki is the exception to avoiding aspirin."),
("Kawasaki","A child received IVIG 2 g/kg for Kawasaki disease. How long should MMR and varicella be delayed?",
 "11 months",["1 month","6 months","They are permanently contraindicated"],
 "\"2 grams → wait 11 months.\" Non-live vaccines do not need to wait."),
# ---- ARF (3)
("Rheumatic fever","A 9-year-old had a sore throat 3 weeks ago and now has migratory knee and ankle arthritis plus a new murmur. ASO is elevated. The most likely diagnosis is:",
 "Acute rheumatic fever",["Kawasaki disease","Septic arthritis","PSGN"],
 "JONES: 2 majors (Joints + carditis) plus evidence of prior strep = ARF."),
("Rheumatic fever","A child has migratory polyarthritis, fever, and elevated ESR. Why is arthralgia NOT counted as a minor criterion here?",
 "Arthralgia cannot be counted when arthritis is already a major criterion",["Arthralgia is a major criterion","Arthralgia only counts in adults","Arthralgia requires an ASO titer"],
 "Don't double-count: arthritis + arthralgia, or carditis + prolonged PR."),
("Rheumatic fever","The long-term cardiac damage from rheumatic fever most often affects the:",
 "Mitral (and aortic) valves",["Coronary arteries","Pericardium","Conduction system only"],
 "Rheumatic = Valves. Kawasaki = Coronaries."),
# ---- Other cardiac (3)
("Other cardiac","A child has sharp chest pain that worsens lying flat and improves leaning forward. A friction rub is heard. The nurse suspects:",
 "Pericarditis",["Myocarditis","Costochondritis","Aortic stenosis"],
 "Pericarditis = \"praying position\" relief. Assess for effusion/tamponade."),
("Other cardiac","Which child needs dental endocarditis prophylaxis for a qualifying procedure?",
 "A child with a prosthetic heart valve",["A child with a small isolated VSD","A child with an uncomplicated ASD","A child with mitral valve prolapse alone"],
 "Prophylaxis is limited to the highest-risk patients (prosthetic valve, prior IE, selected cyanotic/repaired CHD)."),
("Other cardiac","A tall teen with long fingers, pectus excavatum, and a dislocated lens likely has Marfan syndrome. Which cardiac concern is the priority?",
 "Aortic root dilation and dissection",["Coronary aneurysm","Coarctation","Tetralogy of Fallot"],
 "Marfan → aortic root aneurysm/dissection, plus possible MVP."),
# ---- Meds (6)
("Dose calculation","A child weighs 22 kg. Acetaminophen is ordered at 15 mg/kg/dose. The suspension is 160 mg/5 mL. How many mL per dose?",
 "10.3 mL",["6.9 mL","15 mL","3.3 mL"],
 "22 × 15 = 330 mg. 330 × 5 ÷ 160 = 10.3 mL."),
("Dose calculation","An 18-kg child is ordered ibuprofen 10 mg/kg/dose. The suspension is 100 mg/5 mL. How many mL?",
 "9 mL",["3.6 mL","18 mL","4.5 mL"],
 "18 × 10 = 180 mg. 180 × 5 ÷ 100 = 9 mL."),
("Dose calculation","A 44-lb child with AOM is ordered amoxicillin 90 mg/kg/day divided BID. How many mg per dose?",
 "900 mg",["1,800 mg","1,980 mg","450 mg"],
 "44 ÷ 2.2 = 20 kg. 20 × 90 = 1,800 mg/day ÷ 2 = 900 mg per dose. \"Per DAY → Divide.\""),
("Dose calculation","A 25-kg child with GAS is ordered cephalexin 20 mg/kg/dose BID. What is the correct amount?",
 "500 mg per dose, twice daily",["500 mg per day total","250 mg per dose, twice daily","1,000 mg per dose"],
 "\"Per DOSE → Don't divide.\" 25 × 20 = 500 mg each dose (max 500 mg/dose)."),
("Medications","What is the minimum age for routine ibuprofen use, and its dosing interval?",
 "6 months; every 6–8 hours",["2 months; every 4 hours","Newborn; every 4–6 hours","12 months; every 12 hours"],
 "Ibuprofen = \"the 6s\": 6 months and older, every 6–8 hours. Acetaminophen is every 4–6 hours."),
("Medications","Why is aspirin avoided as a routine fever reducer in children with viral illness?",
 "Risk of Reye syndrome",["It causes bronchospasm in all children","It is ineffective for fever","It causes kidney stones"],
 "Reye syndrome risk. Kawasaki disease is a specialist-directed exception."),
# ---- Immunizations (13)
("Immunizations","Which vaccines are routinely given at the 2-month visit?",
 "DTaP, IPV, Hib, PCV, and rotavirus",["MMR, varicella, and HepA","Tdap, MenACWY, and HPV","Influenza and MMR"],
 "2, 4, 6 months: \"Dad Is Having Pizza Rolls\" = DTaP, IPV, Hib, PCV, Rotavirus (plus HepB timing)."),
("Immunizations","Which live vaccines are first given routinely at 12–15 months?",
 "MMR and varicella",["DTaP and IPV","Rotavirus and HepB","Tdap and HPV"],
 "The \"live birthday party\" at 12–15 months: MMR + varicella (plus Hib/PCV boosters and HepA)."),
("Immunizations","A 5-year-old is entering kindergarten. Which boosters are due at 4–6 years?",
 "DTaP, IPV, MMR, and varicella",["Tdap, HPV, MenACWY","HepB, Hib, PCV","MenB and influenza only"],
 "School entry = \"DIMV.\""),
("Immunizations","Which vaccines are routinely given at 11–12 years?",
 "Tdap, MenACWY, and HPV",["DTaP, IPV, and MMR","MMR and varicella","Hib and PCV"],
 "\"Teens Meet HPV.\" HPV may start at age 9; MenACWY booster at 16."),
("Immunizations","A 16-week-old infant has never received rotavirus vaccine. What should the nurse do?",
 "Do not start rotavirus; the first dose must be given by 14 weeks 6 days",["Give rotavirus dose 1 today","Give a double dose to catch up","Wait until 6 months to start"],
 "Rotavirus: start by 14 weeks 6 days (\"by 15 weeks\"), finish by 8 months."),
("Immunizations","A child received MMR. Two weeks later, varicella vaccine was given. How should the nurse interpret the varicella dose?",
 "It is not valid; injectable live vaccines must be given the same day or at least 28 days apart",["It is valid; live vaccines can be given at any interval","It is valid because MMR and varicella are different viruses","Only the MMR needs to be repeated"],
 "\"Live shots: same day or wait a month (28 days).\" The second live vaccine given too early should be repeated."),
("Immunizations","A 3-year-old received DTaP doses 1 and 2 as an infant, then missed a year. What is the correct plan?",
 "Continue with dose 3; do not restart the series",["Restart the series from dose 1","Give Tdap instead","No more doses are needed"],
 "Catch-up: NEVER restart a series. DTaP is used under 7 years."),
("Immunizations","A 12-month-old has a mild cold (afebrile) and is taking amoxicillin for AOM. Vaccines are due. The nurse should:",
 "Give the scheduled vaccines today",["Defer until the antibiotic course is done","Defer all live vaccines for 1 month","Give only non-live vaccines"],
 "Mild illness and antibiotics are not contraindications. Moderate/severe illness is a precaution."),
("Immunizations","A 12-year-old got HPV dose 1, and dose 2 was given 4 months later. What is needed?",
 "Another dose, because doses less than 5 months apart are not valid for the 2-dose series",["Nothing; the series is complete","Restart the series","Two more doses"],
 "Started before 15 = 2 doses 6–12 months apart; if less than 5 months apart, a third dose is needed."),
("Immunizations","A 16-year-old is starting the HPV series. How many doses are needed?",
 "3 doses",["1 dose","2 doses","4 doses"],
 "Starting at age 15 or older (or immunocompromised) = 3 doses."),
("Immunizations","A 20-kg child took prednisone 2 mg/kg/day for 3 weeks and stopped yesterday. MMR is due. The nurse should:",
 "Wait at least 1 month after stopping before giving MMR",["Give MMR today","Never give MMR to this child","Give MMR at half dose"],
 "Steroid \"2–20–14\": 2 mg/kg/day or 20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more → delay live vaccines at least 1 month."),
("Immunizations","A healthy 12-month-old lives with a grandparent receiving chemotherapy. Regarding MMR and varicella, the nurse should:",
 "Give both vaccines as scheduled",["Withhold both until the grandparent finishes chemo","Give MMR only","Give varicella only"],
 "Household immunocompromise is not a reason to withhold MMR/varicella. If a varicella rash develops, avoid direct contact until it resolves."),
("Immunizations","An infant received MMR at 9 months before international travel. What is the plan?",
 "Still give 2 routine MMR doses starting at 12 months",["The travel dose counts as dose 1","No further MMR doses are needed","Give one more dose at 4–6 years only"],
 "A dose before 12 months does not count toward the routine 2-dose series."),
# ---- School / teaching (3)
("School and teaching","A child with chickenpox may return to school when:",
 "All lesions are crusted over",["5 days have passed since the rash appeared","The fever has been gone for 24 hours","No new lesions appear for 2 hours"],
 "Varicella: all lesions crusted. A fixed number of days alone is not enough."),
("School and teaching","Which instruction is correct for a child's nosebleed?",
 "Sit up, lean forward, and pinch the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes",["Tilt the head back and pinch the bridge","Lie flat with ice on the forehead","Pinch for 1 minute, then check"],
 "Lean FORWARD and pinch the soft alae continuously for at least 10 minutes. Never tilt back."),
("School and teaching","A child with fifth disease (erythema infectiosum) has the classic rash and feels well. Regarding school:",
 "No exclusion is needed for the rash alone",["Exclude until the rash fades","Exclude for 5 days","Exclude until 24 hours of antibiotics"],
 "Infectivity is greatest BEFORE the rash, so there is no exclusion once the child is well. Special precautions apply for pregnancy/immunocompromise."),
]
